From: ElementalWarrior
Date: 2026-10-12
Subject: Submit the queue in clFlush instead of dropping it

Restore the real clFlush thunk; a no-op Flush stalls the GPU queue.

0030 turned clFlush into `return CL_SUCCESS` on the theory that Flush is
only a hint. In practice the host driver batches enqueued commands and
Flush is what submits them: Affinity enqueues its startup compute work,
registers a completion callback and waits for it. With nothing submitted
the event never completes and the wait never ends, so OpenCL has to stay
off entirely and Affinity loses GPU acceleration.

Submit the queue for real and then pump any callbacks that are already
due, so a Flush/Wait pair makes forward progress on its own. The thunk
is one PE->unix round trip; the SRWLOCK/SwitchToThread work from 0030 is
kept.

--- a/dlls/opencl/pe_thunks.c	2026-10-12 09:30:20.132578436 -0300
+++ b/dlls/opencl/pe_thunks.c	2026-10-12 09:30:20.133515270 -0300
@@ -463,8 +463,12 @@ cl_int WINAPI clFinish( cl_command_queue
 
 cl_int WINAPI clFlush( cl_command_queue command_queue )
 {
+    struct clFlush_params params = { command_queue };
+    cl_int ret;
     TRACE( "(%p)\n", command_queue );
-    return CL_SUCCESS;
+    ret = OPENCL_CALL( clFlush, &params );
+    check_completed_callbacks();
+    return ret;
 }
 
 cl_int WINAPI clGetCommandQueueInfo( cl_command_queue command_queue, cl_command_queue_info param_name, size_t param_value_size, void* param_value, size_t* param_value_size_ret )
